    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Folding and fracturing are intimately related processes with complex interactions between the remote stress, local folding related stresses and pre-existing fractures all influencing new fracture formation. The first steps to figuring out these relationships are documenting existing examples of folding induced and related fractures and designing conceptual models based on these data which explain the initiation and propagation of fracture sets and include the effects of pre-existing structures. Here, field data from Raplee Monocline in SE Utah and conceptual models of fracture evolution based on these data are presented. Five stages of fracturing are described: 1) the formation of pre-folding E-W Set I fractures by regional stress, 2) the formation of pre-folding N-S Set II fractures probably by regional stress, 3) the formation of tail-cracks off Set II and the flow of fluids through these and the main Set II fractures, 4) right lateral shear induced by folding creating NW-SE Set III fractures as tail-cracks of Set I fractures and as independent fractures and 5) the formation of Set II fractures as tail-cracks from slip along bedding planes.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
